      "heading": "C - Rules of the Road dossier",
      "learner_facing": true,
      "markdown": "# C - Rules of the Road dossier\n\nDomain: rules of the road covering flex lanes, freeway use, express lanes, interchanges, intersections, markings, speed, roundabouts, signals, signs, and right of way.\n\nFACT_ID: C-01 | anchor: A01\nFlex lane direction is controlled by overhead signals with green arrow to use and red X to avoid.\nWhy it matters: lane direction can reverse by time of day so driver must check signal before entering.\nSource: [A01 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0049]\n\nFACT_ID: C-02 | anchor: A01\nFreeway following, U-turn, emergency lane, ramp use and impeding rules govern safe freeway travel.\nWhy it matters: U-turns, emergency-lane travel, and entrance-ramp exits are prohibited and left-lane impeding can draw citation.\nSource: [A01 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0049]\n\nFACT_ID: C-03 | anchor: A02\nExpress lane free use covers two-or-more occupants plus motorcycles, buses, and registered C-decal clean-fuel vehicles.\nWhy it matters: single-occupant use without pass or decal is a violation and a common exam trap.\nSource: [A02 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0050]\n\nFACT_ID: C-04 | anchor: A02\nDouble white line crossing is prohibited and express entry or exit is allowed only at dotted access points.\nWhy it matters: access segments are limited in length and located near interchanges, so early exit planning is required.\nSource: [A02 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0050]\n\nFACT_ID: C-05 | anchor: A02\nC decal use follows one-time registration payment and windshield placement to the right of the mirror.\nWhy it matters: only qualifying clean-fuel vehicles may use express lanes toll-free on a single-occupant basis.\nSource: [A02 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0050]\n\nFACT_ID: C-06 | anchor: A02\nMulti-lane entry uses acceleration lane to match speed then merge and cancel signal, while exit uses early lane choice and steady speed.\nWhy it matters: late lane changes and speed drops on the main road create rear-end risk.\nSource: [A02 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0050]\n\nFACT_ID: C-07 | anchor: A03\nRamp meters show alternating red and green and normally release one vehicle per green unless posted otherwise.\nWhy it matters: metering is active only when meter-on sign is lit, otherwise drivers enter normally.\nSource: [A03 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0051]\n\nFACT_ID: C-08 | anchor: A03\nRunaway ramps are for emergency stopping only with no stopping, standing, or parking in ramp or pathway.\nWhy it matters: blocking a runaway path removes the last safety option for a brake-failure vehicle.\nSource: [A03 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0051]\n\nFACT_ID: C-09 | anchor: A03\nSingle-point interchange U-turn from off-ramp to on-ramp is lawful on green arrow and completed without mid-intersection stop.\nWhy it matters: the adjacent red arrow faces other traffic, and power loss calls for extreme caution not four-way-stop behavior.\nSource: [A03 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0051]\n\nFACT_ID: C-10 | anchor: A04\nIntersection approach requires scanning controls and users, full stop behind signs and lines, and no lane change inside.\nWhy it matters: cross traffic may not yield, so left-right-left check and clear-path confirmation are essential.\nSource: [A04 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0052]\n\nFACT_ID: C-11 | anchor: A05\nContinuous-flow left uses displaced crossover on signal, while right turns yield and may face restricted red-turn permission.\nWhy it matters: drivers must watch two left signals and obey posted right-turn controls rather than assuming free right.\nSource: [A05 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0053]\n\nFACT_ID: C-12 | anchor: A05\nDiverging diamond briefly shifts non-freeway traffic to the opposite side to cut left-turn conflicts at ramps.\nWhy it matters: unfamiliar alignment demands lane discipline and attention to guide markings.\nSource: [A05 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0053]\n\nFACT_ID: C-13 | anchor: A05\nThru-U-turn removes direct lefts by sending drivers through, then to a signalized U-turn and back for a right.\nWhy it matters: congestion relief depends on drivers accepting longer path instead of turning at the main junction.\nSource: [A05 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0053]\n\nFACT_ID: C-14 | anchor: A06\nYellow center markings separate opposite travel and control passing, while white markings separate same-direction flow.\nWhy it matters: dashed-side permission and double-line prohibition determine lawful passing and express separation.\nSource: [A06 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0054]\n\nFACT_ID: C-15 | anchor: A06\nUnmarked crosswalks still require stop before the intersection and yield to entering pedestrians.\nWhy it matters: stop line, crosswalk, or intersection edge defines the stop point at signs and signals.\nSource: [A06 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0054]\n\nFACT_ID: C-16 | anchor: A06\nShared center left lane entry is limited in distance before the turn and requires mirror, signal, and blind-spot check.\nWhy it matters: early entry turns the shared lane into unlawful travel and surprises oncoming left-turn users.\nSource: [A06 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0054]\n\nFACT_ID: C-17 | anchor: A07\nBasic speed law bars unreasonable speed for conditions, with posted school, business, highway, and rural interstate tiers.\nWhy it matters: listed limits apply only absent signs or hazards, and poor weather or narrow roads demand slower travel.\nSource: [A07 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0055]\n\nFACT_ID: C-18 | anchor: A08\nSignal and sign hierarchy places officer commands first, then flashing and steady indications, with work-zone fines doubled.\nWhy it matters: green does not override an officer stop order, dark signals need full stop and yield, and flaggers must be obeyed.\nSource: [A08 -> src:rdmv-ut-class-d:utah-dld:handbook:pdf-0056]\n\nHigh-yield focus: signals and right of way first, then speed and markings, then freeway and express operations.\n\nMisconceptions:\nLeft lane camping is acceptable when at the limit; correction is move right for overtaking traffic.\nRight on red is always allowed; correction is obey posted bans and continuous-flow controls.\nCrossing a double white to leave express is minor; correction is wait for dotted access.\nUnmarked crosswalks grant no pedestrian right; correction is yield even without paint.\nSlow driving is always safe; correction is avoid impeding normal flow.\n\nMnemonics:\nGreen means go-check, yellow means soon-red, flashing yellow means careful-yield, flashing red means stop-look.\nFlex check: arrow overhead before tire over line.\nExpress doping: dotted door only, never double white.\n\nGlossary:\nFlex lane: time-controlled shared-direction lane governed by overhead signals.\nExpress lane: high-occupancy corridor with controlled dotted access.\nAccess point: dotted segment where express entry or exit is permitted.\nRamp meter: alternating ramp signal regulating freeway entry flow.\nShared center lane: middle two-way left-turn lane, not a travel lane.\nDiverging diamond: interchange briefly shifting sides to reduce left conflicts.",
